The good news is that your **backend pagination is already perfectly correct**. In VoucherQueryService.java, you are using Oracle's OFFSET ? ROWS FETCH NEXT ? ROWS ONLY with the correct math (offset = page * size). It is rock solid.
The issue is entirely in the frontend. When you change the dropdown from 10 to 25, the paginationModel state updates, but because that state is missing from the React useCallback dependency array, the fetchMyBatches function never realizes it needs to re-fetch the new data from the server.
Here are the exact two replacements to fix this in **VoucherPostingStatus.js**:
### 1. Fix the fetchMyBatches Dependency Array
Scroll down to the very end of your fetchMyBatches function. We need to add the pagination state to the dependency array so React knows to trigger a new API call whenever the page or size changes.
**Replace this (around line 345):**
```javascript
    } finally {
      if (isMounted.current) setLoading(false);
    }
  }, [callApi, showSnackBar]);

```
**With this:**
```javascript
    } finally {
      if (isMounted.current) setLoading(false);
    }
  }, [callApi, showSnackBar, paginationModel.page, paginationModel.pageSize]);

```
### 2. Add 50 to the Page Size Dropdown
You mentioned wanting to select 50, but your DataGrid options currently stop at 25. Let's add 50 to the array.
**Find your <DataGrid> component near the bottom of the file and replace this:**
```javascript
        <DataGrid
          rows={rows}
          columns={columns}
          loading={loading}
          getRowId={(row) => row.batchId}
          disableRowSelectionOnClick
          pageSizeOptions={[5, 10, 25]}
          disableColumnResize
          rowHeight={52}

```
**With this:**
```javascript
        <DataGrid
          rows={rows}
          columns={columns}
          loading={loading}
          getRowId={(row) => row.batchId}
          disableRowSelectionOnClick
          pageSizeOptions={[5, 10, 25, 50]} // <--- Added 50 here
          disableColumnResize
          rowHeight={52}

```
Now, whenever you change the rows per page to 25 or 50, the useEffect will instantly detect the change, pass the new sizes directly into your &size= API URL, and your Oracle backend will seamlessly return the exact chunk of data you requested.
